>
  {/* ---------------- OVERVIEW ---------------- */}
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    This was a 5+ week project for a UC San Diego course,
    <em> Listening to the Future: Speculating Through Soundscapes and
    Sonification</em>. The brief: based on real research, argue for a
    plausible future 30&ndash;40 years from now that can be understood
    <em>through sound</em> &mdash; utopian, alarmist, or somewhere in
    between.
  </p>
  <p>
    My group took the <strong>alarmist</strong> position, and our thesis was a
    single unsettling idea: <strong>the end of silence.</strong> We imagined a
    future where the unchecked growth of technology and music has saturated the
    world so completely that nature&rsquo;s sounds &mdash; and its silence
    &mdash; are lost. In trying to escape our own anxiety and boredom, we reach
    for technology that comforts and entertains us, but ultimately controls us,
    until we forget the natural world entirely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>I was the project lead.</strong> I steered the concept, ran and
    synthesized the research, drafted the proposal, designed the piece&rsquo;s
    structure, and did the actual audio editing and production of the final
    soundscape.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- LISTEN ---------------- */}
  <h2>Listen</h2>
  <p>
    The finished piece is a ~3:45 multi-tracked soundscape &mdash; a day in the
    life of an introverted man who uses music as a drug to manage his anxiety,
    in a world where silence no longer exists. Best with headphones.
  </p>
  <audio controls preload="none" src="/projects/sound-speculation/sound-speculation.mp3"
  ></audio>

  {/* ---------------- THE GRAPHIC SCORE ---------------- */}
  <h2>The graphic score</h2>
  <p>
    Before touching the audio, I mapped the entire piece as a
    <strong> graphic score</strong> &mdash; a visual blueprint laying out, across
    the timeline, every section&rsquo;s narrative, the specific sounds used, a
    graphic notation of the sound itself, and the editing effects that would
    carry each moment. It doubled as the production plan I edited against and as
    the team&rsquo;s shared reference.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/sound-speculation/graphic-score.png"
      alt="A wide graphic score for the soundscape. A time axis runs from 0 to 3:45, split into two acts — 'Home' (dream about nature, using music as a drug, starting day) and 'Outside' (entering outside, drive to work, work → end). Rows below map, for each section, a description, the specific sounds used, a graphic notation of the sound, and the editing effects (reverb, distortion, looping, amplification, overlapping clips)."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The graphic score &mdash; two acts (Home &rarr; Outside), with sounds,
      notation, and editing effects mapped across the full timeline.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The structure follows the character across a single morning. In the{" "}
    <strong>Home</strong> act he dreams of nature (a past when it still
    existed), wakes to crippling anxiety, and reaches for his headphones &mdash;
    music as a drug &mdash; looping the same song over and over into a trance.
    In the <strong>Outside</strong> act he opens his door into a wall of
    overlapping media and overstimulation, drives to a volunteer shift at a
    concert, and the piece builds to a dystopian climax: birds falling from a
    sky that has no room left for them, and a fading voice saying
    <em> &ldquo;I miss there being nature.&rdquo;</em>
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- TECHNICAL CRAFT ---------------- */}
  <h2>Producing the piece</h2>
  <p>
    The editing is where the concept had to actually become <em>audible</em>.
    A few of the production choices I leaned on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Headphones on / off as a POV switch.</strong> Reverb, muffling,
      and drawing sound out let me flip between the character&rsquo;s calm
      inner mind (music on) and the harsh outside world &mdash; the two spaces
      the whole story turns on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Looping as addiction.</strong> The same track repeats, and
      repeats, and repeats &mdash; the edit itself performs the overreliance
      the piece is arguing about.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Overlapping clips = no more silence.</strong> Layering dense,
      overlapping audio &mdash; radios, notifications, TikToks, crowd noise
      &mdash; so that in the Outside act there is never a moment of true
      quiet, which is the thesis made literal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Distortion and rising amplitude for the climax.</strong>{" "}
      Gradual distortion, a pounding heartbeat, and volume creeping up on a
      button-press build the overstimulation toward the ending.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The course also framed sound through Schafer&rsquo;s
    <em> Apollonian vs. Dionysian</em> lens &mdash; ordered, designed sound
    versus chaotic, overwhelming sound &mdash; which is exactly the tension the
    piece rides from the quiet dream at the start to the chaos at the end.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- RESEARCH ---------------- */}
  <h2>The research behind it</h2>
  <p>
    The speculation is grounded in real sources. As lead I pulled together
    three research threads from the team and tied them to one argument &mdash;
    that the growth of technology reshapes both people and the environment:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Music, anxiety, and dependence.</strong> Research on music
      reducing anxiety and pain (and on performance anxiety) &mdash; extended
      to a future where that comfort curdles into overreliance, replacing human
      support.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ecomusicology and the environment.</strong> Work on music&rsquo;s
      material impact and ecological cost &mdash; the idea that silence is
      itself musical, and that the environment is being drowned out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Modernizing tradition.</strong> LIV Golf bolting concerts and
      spectacle onto a traditional sport &mdash; a small, real example of
      technology and entertainment overwriting older, quieter practices.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The full write-up &mdash; proposal, guiding research questions, speculative
    trajectory, and the annotated bibliography &mdash; is here:
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/projects/sound-speculation/group-proposal.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Read the proposal &amp; annotated bibliography (PDF) ↗
    </a>
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- REFLECTION ---------------- */}
  <h2>What I took away</h2>
  <p>
    This one sits outside my usual engineering work, and that&rsquo;s exactly
    why I like having it here. Leading it meant turning an abstract argument
    into a concrete artifact &mdash; holding a thesis steady across research,
    a written proposal, a visual score, and finally an edited piece of audio
    where every effect had to <em>mean</em> something. It&rsquo;s the same
    muscle as shipping software: decide what you&rsquo;re saying, design how the
    pieces fit, and make sure the final thing actually lands with whoever
    experiences it.
  </p>
